\section{Relate Clocks Instead of Choosing One Master}

SIGNAL's polychronous model describes values and relations among the instants at which signals are present. Components need not begin with one universal clock. Equations constrain clock equality, subclocking, union, and exclusion; compilation solves those relations and may discover several independent clock domains. A feasible implementation still needs communication protocols where domains interact.

This differs from treating absence as a stored boolean flag at every global tick. A signal has values only on its clock. The \texttt{when} operator samples on a condition's true occurrences, \texttt{default} combines signals by presence priority, and delay carries the previous present value on its relevant clock.

\section{State Clock Relations in SIGNAL Syntax}

A representative SIGNAL process samples and retains values:

\begin{verbatim}
process SAMPLE =
  (? integer x; boolean c;
   ! integer y; integer held; )
  (| y := x when c
   | held := x $ init 0
   | x ^= c
  |)
where
end;
\end{verbatim}

The clock-equality relation \texttt{x \^{}= c} requires \texttt{x} and \texttt{c} to be present together; \texttt{y} is present only when that shared-clock condition is true. The delay for \texttt{held} advances on occurrences of \texttt{x}, not on an invented faster master clock. \Cref{fig:visual-363} shows the derived clock inclusion.

