% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Solve Before Scheduling}

\begin{verbatim}
constraints := collect_clock_relations(equations)
classes := unify_clock_equalities(constraints)
derive_subclock_and_exclusion_relations(classes)
if relations are inconsistent: reject program
partition equations into clock domains
for each domain:
    schedule instantaneous dependencies
insert protocol only at a proven cross-domain relation
\end{verbatim}

Clock solving establishes when equations may react. It does not impose a total order between independent domains, and an arbitrary total order must not become externally observable.

\section{Keep Polychrony in the IR}

The IR should contain relation/operator nodes and signal-dependency edges, delay state with initialization, symbolic clock variables and solved equality/subclock relations, value types and effects, and source provenance for equations and inferred constraints. Rates may be unknown or nonperiodic; do not replace clock relations by numeric frequencies unless separately proved. Compilation can produce clock-guarded C tasks, a multiclock circuit, or a distributed schedule plus synchronization code.

Legal optimizations include fusing clock-equivalent equations, removing redundant clock tests after proof, and running independent domains in parallel. Semantic-preservation risks include assigning a total order that changes effect order, sampling on condition presence rather than truth, advancing delay on absent instants, and crossing domains without buffering. Preservation requires the same partial-clock traces and relation constraints, not merely the same values after resampling everything onto a convenient test clock.

\section{Build a Trace With Both Absence and Falsehood}

The equality \verb|x ^= c| requires \texttt{x} and \texttt{c} to be present together. The clock of \texttt{y} is smaller: it selects only those occurrences of \texttt{c} whose value is true. The clock of \texttt{held} is that of \texttt{x}; it reports the previous present input rather than holding a value out on a wire at all instants. A test fixture may express these constraints in rows without claiming that independent domains share a physical clock.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{crlrr}
\hline
Fixture row & x & c & y & held \\
\hline
0 & 8 & false & --- & 0 \\
1 & --- & absent & --- & --- \\
2 & 11 & true & 11 & 8 \\
3 & 14 & false & --- & 11 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
presence := present(x)
require presence == present(c)
if not presence:
    emit(Absent, Absent)
else:
    old := memory
    sampled := Present(x.value) if c.value else Absent
    emit(sampled, Present(old))
    memory := x.value
\end{verbatim}

Initialize \texttt{memory} to zero. At row 0 the selector is false, but memory advances because its own clock occurs. At row 1 neither equation advances. Both rows yield an absent \texttt{y}; inspecting \texttt{held} distinguishes their effects. At row 2 the returned delay must still be \(8\). A mistaken lowering that updates memory before emitting the delayed value instead prints \(11\). A fixture that repeatedly sends the same number cannot detect that ordering error.

In a wider SIGNAL process the solver may find independent clock classes. A backend that puts everything into one host loop can simulate each class with explicit presence flags, but it must not introduce extra relations just because the flags are available simultaneously in the host. If external IO ties two classes together, put the corresponding synchronization in the boundary contract and test it there.

\paragraph{Validate an inconsistent input separately.}
Give the harness a row with \texttt{x} present but \texttt{c} absent.
It violates the declared equality; substituting false for the absent
value would hide the configuration error and advance the delay anyway.
For static connections, report the two equated clocks and the boundary
facts that conflict. For dynamic inputs, retain a check at admission.
